        timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine. The M111 utilizes an incredibly robust duplex (double-row) timing chain. Unlike modern engines, this timing system is massively over-engineered and practically indestructible. It rarely requires replacement or even servicing before 250,000 miles (400,000 km), provided basic oil change intervals are respected.)",
        injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Bosch HFM (Hot-Film Mass Air Flow). The US-market C 230 was introduced to replace the C 220, utilizing the highly reliable Bosch HFM system. It utilizes a traditional Mass Air Flow sensor, making it vastly more reliable, easier to diagnose, and completely immune to the fatal internal MAP sensor meltdowns that killed the smaller European pre-facelift engines.)",
        engineLifespan: "300,000+ Miles / 500,000+ km (Mechanically, the 2.3L M111 cast-iron engine block is legendary. Because it avoids the horrific PMS engine management system and was introduced after Mercedes phased out the biodegradable wiring harness (for the US market), the powertrain is incredibly reliable. The engine will easily outlast the chassis. The economic lifespan is heavily threatened by severe structural chassis rust, even in dry states.)",
        turboType: "Naturally Aspirated (No turbocharger or supercharger. Power delivery is extremely linear, smooth, and highly durable. The 2.3L displacement provides excellent mid-range torque, making it a highly capable commuter for American highways.)",
        ecuType: "Bosch HFM (A massive leap forward in reliability. It separately manages fuel injection and ignition using standard sensors. This specific US/Canada variant features strict OBD-II emissions mapping required by federal law, utilizing dual O2 sensors. While tuning potential is essentially zero on the naturally aspirated 2.3L, the ECU is rock-solid.)",
        euroStandard: "US EPA Tier 1 / OBD-II Compliant",
        maxPowerStockInternals: "350+ hp (The iron block and forged internals of the 2.3L M111 are famously overbuilt. The bottom end can handle massive boost. However, tuning the Bosch HFM natively yields almost no gains. Adding forced induction requires a standalone ECU like Megasquirt, which is popular in Europe but extremely rare in North America for this chassis.)",
