    yearRange: "2008-2010",
    timingType: "Chain (Located at the FRONT of the engine block. Even though this is a downsized 1.6-liter version, the M271 utilizes the exact same catastrophic single-row (simplex) timing chain as the 1.8-liter blocks. This single-row chain is incredibly prone to stretching prematurely due to the stress of the variable valve timing system and supercharger load. When the chain stretches, it rapidly machines and destroys the teeth on the incredibly expensive Variable Valve Timing (VVT) camshaft gears. If the cold-start rattle is ignored, the chain will skip teeth, causing catastrophic piston-to-valve contact. It MUST be replaced preventatively every 80 000 to 100 000 kilometers along with the tensioner to avoid total engine destruction.)",
    injectionType: "Manifold Injection / Siemens SIM4 LKE (A highly advanced sequential electronic fuel injection system utilizing a Hot-Film Mass Air Flow (MAF) sensor. While the fuel injection system itself is reliable, it is constantly victimized by external factors. The most notorious inherited issue is the camshaft adjuster magnets leaking engine oil directly into the engine wiring harness. Through capillary action, this oil travels all the way into the Siemens ECU and the Lambda sensors, destroying the entire electronic injection control system if isolation pigtails are not installed.)",
    engineLifespan: "250 000 - 350 000+ km (The mechanical lifespan of the all-aluminum M271 engine block is entirely dictated by the owner's vigilance regarding the timing chain. If the timing chain and camshaft gears are replaced exactly when needed, the engine bottom end will last a very long time. The W204 chassis surrounding it is a massive upgrade over the W203, entirely eliminating the catastrophic rust issues. However, the vehicle's ultimate demise is almost always caused by neglected timing chains snapping or the electronic steering lock immobilizing the vehicle permanently.)",
    turboType: "Supercharged (Equipped with a continuously driven Eaton M45 Roots-type positive displacement supercharger. It spins constantly with the engine, providing absolutely seamless and instantaneous throttle response with zero lag, which is critical for making the small 1.6-liter feel punchy. However, because it spins constantly, the internal planetary gears wear out rapidly. The specialized aviation oil inside the supercharger snout must be changed every 100 000 kilometers, or the bearings will run dry, causing the supercharger to emit a horrific grinding noise before seizing entirely.)",
    ecuType: "Siemens SIM4 LKE (A highly complex engine management system mapped specifically for the BlueEFFICIENCY fuel economy targets. The ECU integrates with a smart alternator and a decoupled power steering pump to reduce parasitic drag. The ECU is incredibly vulnerable to oil intrusion from the engine wiring harness. If oil reaches the ECU pins from the leaking camshaft magnets, the entire unit will short out and require highly expensive replacement and complex dealership re-coding.)",
    euroStandard: "Euro 4",
    maxPowerStockInternals: "220+ hp (The internal rotating assembly of the downsized 1.6-liter M271 is robust, but it lacks the displacement of the 1.8-liter versions. The true limitation for massive power is the smaller engine volume and the flow capacity of the Eaton M45 supercharger. Tuning this specific 1.6L block yields far less dramatic results than tuning the 1.8L variants.)",
